<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0">
  <displayName>regx administrative template</displayName>
  <description>Constrains what the regx registry CLI may do on this machine.</description>

  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="Cat_Regx">regx</string>

      <string id="AuditLog">Require an audit log</string>
      <string id="AuditLog_Help">Every registry change regx makes is recorded to this file, whether or not the operator passed --audit-log.

Records are hash-chained: altering or removing one breaks the chain from that point, and "regx audit &lt;file&gt;" reports where. Point this at a location users can append to but not rewrite, and ship it somewhere append-only if the trail has to survive a determined local administrator.

If disabled or not configured, logging happens only when the operator asks for it.</string>

      <string id="AuditRedact">Redact values in the audit log</string>
      <string id="AuditRedact_Help">Records the SHA-256 and byte length of each value instead of the value itself, and elides value data from the recorded command line.

Enable this where registry values hold licence keys, tokens or connection strings — without it the audit log becomes a secret in its own right. It is still enough to prove a specific value was written, or to compare two runs.

If disabled or not configured, the operator decides with --audit-redact.</string>

      <string id="MinConfidence">Minimum redirection confidence</string>
      <string id="MinConfidence_Help">Smart Redirection rewrites HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE paths to their per-user equivalent, grading each mapping. This sets the floor below which a mapping is refused.

High: only mappings Windows itself honours, such as Software\Classes and the per-user Run key.
Medium: also mappings that work when the application checks HKCU. This is regx's own default.
Low: also mappings unlikely to take effect, such as machine policies that Group Policy refresh will overwrite.

The operator may ask for a stricter floor but not a looser one. If not configured, regx uses medium.</string>
      <string id="Conf_High">High — only mappings Windows honours</string>
      <string id="Conf_Medium">Medium — also application-dependent mappings</string>
      <string id="Conf_Low">Low — also mappings unlikely to take effect</string>

      <string id="DenyKeys">Deny writes to these keys</string>
      <string id="DenyKeys_Help">One registry path per line. regx refuses any operation that would write to these keys or anything beneath them, and writes nothing at all rather than applying the rest of a file.

Matching is on whole path components and is case-insensitive, so HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Acme covers Acme and its subkeys but not AcmeOther.

This restricts regx only. It is not an ACL, and does not stop other tools.</string>

      <string id="DisableHive">Disable the offline hive engine</string>
      <string id="DisableHive_Help">Forbids the "regx hive" command, which mounts a hive file through RegLoadAppKey — an API that needs no administrator rights.

Enable this where users must not be able to read or edit hive files belonging to other profiles, backups or mounted images. The rest of regx continues to work.

If disabled or not configured, the hive engine is available and still bounded by NTFS permissions on the file.</string>

      <string id="RequireConfirm">Require confirmation for every write</string>
      <string id="RequireConfirm_Help">Ignores -y, so a human confirms each change interactively.

Enable this where regx must not be usable from an unattended script. Note that --dry-run is never gated, because it does not write.</string>
    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="AuditLog">
        <textBox refId="AuditLogPath">
          <label>Audit log path</label>
          <defaultValue>C:\ProgramData\regx\audit.jsonl</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="MinConfidence">
        <dropdownList refId="MinConfidenceLevel" noSort="true" defaultItem="0">Floor</dropdownList>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="DenyKeys">
        <multiTextBox refId="DenyKeysList">Denied key paths, one per line</multiTextBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
